              "function_response": null,
              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "**AltaVista** was one of the most popular and pioneering early web search engines of the 1990s. \n\nIf you type `altavista.com` into your browser today, you will be redirected to **Yahoo! Search**, which officially shut down the AltaVista service in 2013.\n\n### A Brief History of AltaVista:\n\n* **The Launch (1995):** AltaVista was launched in December 1995 by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC). It was a massive technological breakthrough at the time because it was the first search engine that could index the full text of the World Wide Web quickly, using a fast crawler and advanced hardware.\n* **The Golden Era:** In the late 90s, AltaVista was the undisputed king of search. On its first day, it had about 300,000 hits, and by 1997, it was processing over 80 million searches a day. It also pioneered web technologies we still use today, such as **CAPTCHA** (to prevent bots from spamming the index) and the **Babel Fish** translation service (one of the earliest free online translators).\n* **The Downward Spiral:** In 1998, DEC was bought by Compaq, which didn't quite know what to do with a search engine. They tried to turn AltaVista into a \"web portal\" (similar to Yahoo! at the time), cluttered with news, shopping, and email. This alienated users who just wanted a clean search interface.\n* **The Rise of Google:** Around the same time, Google debuted with a clean homepage and its revolutionary PageRank algorithm, which delivered much more accurate search results. AltaVista rapidly lost market share to Google.\n* **The End of an Era (2003\u20132013):** After changing hands multiple times, AltaVista was acquired by Yahoo! in 2003. Yahoo! kept the brand alive for a decade but eventually decided to shut down several of its legacy services. On **July 8, 2013**, AltaVista was officially laid to rest, and the domain began redirecting to Yahoo Search.",
